    "axes[1].plot(rate.index, rate.values, marker=\"o\", color=\"#eb6834\")\n",
    "axes[1].set_title(\"FC1 missing rate, Phase 2\", fontsize=10)\n",
    "axes[1].set_xlabel(\"day\")\n",
    "fig.tight_layout()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "105773a0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "FC1's gap is one skipped measurement day at the end of Phase 2 - every plant, unrelated to biology. That's **MAR**: predictable from `day` alone.\n",
    "\n",
    "S3D's gap shrinks over Phase 1 as plants grow, then disappears - the scanner needs a big-enough plant to build a point cloud. If so, plants missing an S3D value should look smaller on a sensor that never fails, like VNIR:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "f6cb6ee4",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-09-22T23:05:05.701903Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-09-22T23:05:05.701903Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-09-22T23:05:05.923005Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-09-22T23:05:05.921997Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "p1 = tomato[tomato.Phase == \"P1\"].copy()\n",
    "p1[\"S3D\"] = np.where(p1.PlantHeight.isna(), \"missing\", \"present\")\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6, 3.6))\n",
    "for status, color in [(\"present\", \"#2a78d6\"), (\"missing\", \"#d03b3b\")]:\n",
    "    trend = p1[p1.S3D == status].groupby(\"day\")[\"VNIR_Size\"].median()\n",
    "    ax.plot(trend.index, trend.values, marker=\"o\", color=color, label=f\"S3D {status}\")\n",
    "ax.set_yscale(\"log\")\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel(\"day\"); ax.set_ylabel(\"VNIR_Size (median)\")\n",
    "ax.legend(fontsize=8, frameon=False)\n",
    "fig.tight_layout()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "be19a9c7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Plants with a missing S3D value are smaller on VNIR too, every day - the gap tracks size, not chance. That's **MNAR**: missingness depends on the plant's own size. `VNIR_Size` could fill the gap; median-imputing S3D instead - what `pca_projection` does below - quietly pulls the smallest, most-missing plants up.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Q2.** Does that change how you would read a PCA axis that S3D traits load heavily on, for early Phase-1 days? UMAP?"
